[Marquise diamond earrings](/marquise-cut-diamond) are the stud style that confuses buyers more than any other shape. The CTW label trips up first-time buyers. The orientation is fixed by the setting and can't be adjusted at home. And the elongated stone looks strikingly different from round studs at the same carat weight — which either delights you or surprises you, depending on what you expected.

Get the three core decisions right — CTW, setting, and orientation — and marquise studs are one of the best-value earrings you can buy. The elongated shape delivers more face-up area per carat than round or cushion. The bezel setting protects both fragile pointed tips. And lab options let you buy significantly more stone for the same budget.

This guide covers all five CTW facts, the bezel vs prong decision, and orientation locking. Full price range: $820 to $16,460.

[![Marquise diamond stud earrings bezel setting 0.5 CTW white gold lifestyle](/images/marquise-diamond-stud-earrings-lifestyle.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/jewelry?ComponentIDs=Earring&Diamonds=Dia&stoneShape=marquise&EarringStyles=stud&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=Image)

## What Makes Marquise Diamond Earrings Different

The marquise cut was designed to maximize visual size per carat. That same property makes it unusual in stud earrings.

A 0.5 CTW marquise pair (0.25ct per ear) has a larger face-up area than a 0.5 CTW round pair. The elongated shape spans more of the earlobe vertically. The result is a stud that reads as more visually present than the carat weight suggests.

The directional flash also behaves differently than round studs. A round brilliant scatters light equally in all directions. A marquise scatters light in a directional pattern along the stone's length axis. When the stone is set point-up, that directional flash creates a visual elongation of the earlobe. It's the same flattering effect that vertical drop earrings create.

The bezel setting is the standard choice for marquise stud earrings. Both pointed tips of a marquise are stress-concentrated points. In a ring, V-prongs protect them. In a stud earring, a full bezel wraps both tips in metal. It eliminates the snagging and tip-chip risk that prong earrings carry. You'll see both settings in this guide — the tradeoffs are real.

## The CTW Double Rule

The CTW Double Rule is simple. Whatever CTW number you see on a pair of earrings, divide by two. That's the per-ear stone weight.

- **0.25 CTW** = 0.125ct per ear
- **0.5 CTW** = 0.25ct per ear
- **1 CTW** = 0.5ct per ear
- **2 CTW** = 1ct per ear

This seems obvious once stated. But most buyers who search for "1ct marquise earrings" expect 1ct per ear. What they actually find is a 1 CTW pair — two stones totaling 1ct, each weighing 0.5ct.

The confusion is compounded by how marquise pendants and rings are labeled. A "1ct marquise ring" has a single 1ct stone. A "1 CTW marquise earring set" has two stones that together weigh 1ct. The same "1ct" language describes very different things.

The CTW Double Rule also changes how you think about sizing. Want matching earrings for a [0.5ct marquise pendant](/marquise-diamond-necklace)? You need a 1 CTW stud set — not a 0.5 CTW set.

This matters for your budget calculation too. Two 0.5ct marquise stones cost more than one 1ct marquise stone at equivalent quality. The pair involves two separate diamond purchases, two settings, two certificates. The price per carat is higher in a pair than a solitaire at the same per-stone weight.

**The practical rule:** Double your target per-ear weight to find the right CTW listing. Want 0.5ct of stone per ear? Search for 1 CTW. Want 0.25ct per ear? Search for 0.5 CTW.

## 5 CTW Facts Marquise Diamond Earring Buyers Get Wrong

### CTW Fact 1: CTW Is the Pair Total — Not Per Ear

When the listing says "1 CTW," each stone weighs 0.5ct. When the listing says "0.5 CTW," each stone weighs 0.25ct. This applies to all diamond stud earrings, not just marquise. But it causes the most confusion here. Buyers compare their studs to marquise rings and pendants that use per-stone weights — not pair totals.

The [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) definition of carat total weight (CTW) is the combined weight of all stones in the piece. For a pair of studs, that means both stones together. Always.

If you're shopping as a gift and you want earrings that match the recipient's marquise pendant, use this: find the pendant's carat weight and double it to get the right CTW for matching studs.

### CTW Fact 2: Marquise CTW Looks Larger Than Round CTW at the Same Number

A 0.5 CTW pair of marquise studs looks bigger on the ear than a 0.5 CTW pair of round brilliant studs. This is not perception — it's geometry.

A 0.25ct marquise stone has a larger face-up surface area than a 0.25ct round brilliant. The marquise's elongated shape spans more visible real estate on the earlobe. The round brilliant's circular shape has a smaller diameter at the same weight because it carries more depth.

This makes marquise studs one of the best shapes for buyers who want visible stone presence at a given budget. You get more visual impact per carat than you would from round, cushion, or princess cut studs.

At 0.5 CTW, the difference between marquise and round is enough to notice across a room. At 1 CTW, it's significant.

### CTW Fact 3: Lab Options Save 40–70% at Identical CTW

Every listing in this guide has a lab-grown option on the same product page. The lab stones carry the same [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) or GIA certification as the natural stones. The cut, color, clarity, and carat weight are identical. The price is not.

At 1 CTW (0.5ct per ear), the certified market inventory for natural marquise studs runs $4,610–$5,500 depending on metal. Lab versions at the same CTW run roughly 40–70% less. That savings is significant enough to jump an entire CTW tier — buying a 1.5 CTW lab pair instead of a 1 CTW natural pair at the same budget.

Lab grown diamond studs are not an inferior substitute. The stones are chemically and physically identical to natural. The price difference reflects origin and market dynamics, not quality.

If you are buying studs as everyday earrings — not as heirlooms or investment pieces — the lab option at higher CTW is worth serious consideration.

### CTW Fact 4: The Setting Locks Your Stone Orientation — Permanently

This is the fact that surprises the most buyers. Round studs look the same from every angle. A marquise stud does not.

A marquise stone set in a stud earring is fixed in one orientation relative to the ear. The setting determines whether the stone's long axis runs vertically (point-up, elongating the lobe) or horizontally (wide-axis across the lobe, bolder and modern). You cannot adjust this at home. You cannot rotate the stone. The orientation is set at manufacturing and stays there.

Most marquise stud earrings come point-up (vertical) by default. This is the traditional orientation. The stone points toward the top of the head, the elongating flash runs up and down, and the stud creates a lengthening visual effect on the earlobe.

Some earrings are set horizontal (east-west). These look modern and bold — the wide axis of the marquise fills the lobe horizontally. The effect is more of a statement than the vertical version.

Before you buy, confirm the orientation from the product images. If the listing shows the stone vertical, that's what you're getting. If you want horizontal, find a listing that shows horizontal.

### CTW Fact 5: "1 CTW Studs" and "1ct Pendant" Are Not the Same Purchase

This is the most practical fact in this list. A buyer who owns a [marquise pendant](/marquise-diamond-necklace) set with a 1ct solitaire stone wants earrings that "match." They search for 1 CTW studs expecting a similar stone size per ear.

What they get: two 0.5ct stones — one per ear.

What a 1ct pendant has: a single 1ct stone hanging from a chain.

A 0.5ct marquise stone per ear looks smaller than a 1ct marquise stone on a pendant. This isn't a flaw — it's just how earrings work. But buyers expecting matched visual weight discover this only after the earrings arrive.

The matching rule: if your pendant is 1ct, buy **2 CTW** studs to get 1ct per ear as a true match. That's the investment tier — [2 CTW marquise bezel studs in 14k white gold are $13,200](https://www.bluenile.com/jewelry/earrings/bezel-diamond-stud-earrings-with-2-ctw-marquise-cut-diamonds-in-14k-white-gold-item-297403?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Or buy a [1 CTW pair for $5,200](https://www.bluenile.com/jewelry/earrings/bezel-diamond-stud-earrings-with-1-ctw-marquise-cut-diamonds-in-14k-white-gold-item-297401?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and wear them knowing each stone is 0.5ct — beautiful studs, just not visually equal to a 1ct pendant.

