## The Tip Trap

The Tip Trap is the reason marquise earrings fail in prong settings: both pointed tips of a marquise are exposed to fabric, hair, and impact in ways that round brilliant studs are not.

A round brilliant has no tips. The smooth curved girdle catches nothing. You can put round studs in and forget about them. A marquise has two sharp points — one at the top of the ear, one at the bottom. In a prong setting, both tips extend beyond the metal.

What happens:
- The bottom tip (pointing toward the jaw) catches on turtleneck collars when you remove them
- The top tip (pointing toward the scalp) catches on hair loops and braids
- Both tips are the most stress-concentrated points on the stone — any lateral impact hits there first

The prong style for marquise earrings typically uses 4 prongs: two on the long sides, two more on the sides. None of the standard 4-prong configurations cap both tips fully. A V-prong at each tip would help, but most stud settings don't include them because the bail-equivalent post placement conflicts with true V-tip prong architecture at earring scale.

The bezel solves this at the source. The full metal collar wraps both tips. Nothing catches. Nothing snags. No lateral impact reaches the stone directly.

This is not a theoretical concern. Chipped marquise tips in earrings are one of the more common repair scenarios in fine jewelry. The geometry of the shape and the exposure of the tips in prong settings create a predictable failure mode.

**The rule:** For everyday marquise studs, bezel or nothing. Prong marquise earrings work for occasional wear but not for the gym, the office, or anything involving close contact with fabric.

