## Frequently Asked Questions

### What does CTW mean for marquise diamond earrings?

CTW means carat total weight — the combined weight of both stones in the pair. A 1 CTW earring set has two stones that together weigh 1ct, meaning each stone is 0.5ct. This is the single most important thing to understand when shopping for marquise studs. Always divide the CTW by 2 to get the per-ear stone weight.

### Do marquise diamond earrings look bigger than round studs at the same CTW?

Yes. The marquise cut has a larger face-up surface area than a round brilliant at the same carat weight. A 0.5 CTW marquise pair (0.25ct per ear) spans more of the earlobe than a 0.5 CTW round pair. The elongated shape is visually more present. You see more stone per dollar than with round studs.

### Can you rotate marquise diamond stud earrings to change the orientation?

No. The orientation is fixed by the setting at manufacturing. If the earring is set point-up (stone pointing toward the top of the head), it stays that way. If it's set horizontal, it stays horizontal. Always check product images to confirm orientation before purchasing. You cannot adjust this after the fact.

### What is the difference between a martini, basket, and bezel setting for marquise studs?

A martini setting uses 3 prongs in a V-shaped cup — elegant, maximum light, both marquise tips exposed. A basket uses 4 prongs in a box frame — slightly more enclosed, tips still exposed. A bezel wraps the entire girdle in a continuous metal rim — full tip protection, cleaner profile. For daily wear with a marquise, bezel is the correct choice.

### Why do bezel marquise studs often cost less than prong marquise studs at the same CTW?

At lower CTW tiers, the bezel is slightly cheaper ($820 vs $970 at 0.25 CTW). At 1 CTW, the bezel ($5,200) is $590 less than the prong ($4,610 in the H-I/SI1-SI2 grade, $5,200 in higher grades). The price varies by the specific stone grades used in each setting. In this guide, the prong earrings use H-I/SI1-SI2 graded stones; the bezel earrings use unspecified (typically higher) grades. The near-price parity at 0.5 CTW makes bezel the clear choice — same cost, better protection.

### How much do marquise diamond stud earrings cost?

Bezel marquise studs start at [$820 for 0.25 CTW](https://www.bluenile.com/jewelry/earrings/bezel-diamond-stud-earrings-with-025-ctw-marquise-cut-diamonds-in-14k-white-gold-item-297398?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and reach [$13,500 for 2 CTW in platinum](https://www.bluenile.com/jewelry/earrings/bezel-diamond-stud-earrings-with-2-ctw-marquise-cut-diamonds-in-platinum-item-297415?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Prong studs start at [$970 for 0.25 CTW](https://www.bluenile.com/jewelry/earrings/marquise-cut-diamond-stud-earrings-in-14k-white-gold-1-4-ct-tw-h-i-si1-si2-item-251897?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and reach [$16,460 for 2 CTW in platinum](https://www.bluenile.com/jewelry/earrings/marquise-cut-diamond-stud-earrings-in-platinum-2-ct-tw-h-i-si1-si2-item-251913?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Lab options are available on each product page at significantly lower prices.

### What CTW should I buy for marquise diamond earrings?

For average earlobes and daily wear, 0.5 CTW (0.25ct per ear) is the most popular choice. It's visible at conversational distance and the marquise shape is clearly readable. For a statement pair or special occasions, 1 CTW (0.5ct per ear) is the milestone tier. For maximum presence, 2 CTW (1ct per ear) matches a 1ct solitaire pendant per ear.

### Are lab-grown marquise diamond earrings the same quality as natural?

Yes. Lab-grown diamonds are chemically and physically identical to natural diamonds — 100% carbon in a diamond crystal structure. The GIA and IGI grade lab stones on the same cut, color, clarity, and carat standards as natural. The price is lower because the origin is a controlled environment rather than a mine. For earrings worn daily rather than held as investments, lab makes strong financial sense.

### Why are marquise tips a problem in prong stud earrings?

The two pointed tips of a marquise stone are stress-concentrated points. In prong stud earrings, these tips extend beyond the metal prongs and are exposed. The bottom tip (pointing toward the jaw) snags on collars and turtlenecks. The top tip catches on hair loops. Any lateral impact hits the tips first. Over time, tip chips are one of the most common repairs for marquise studs in prong settings. The bezel eliminates this risk entirely by wrapping both tips in metal.

### Do marquise diamond earrings go with all outfits?

Yes. The elongated shape and the vertical orientation (point-up) create a subtle lengthening effect on the earlobe that flatters all face shapes. At 0.25–0.5 CTW, the studs are understated enough for casual wear. At 1 CTW and above, they lean toward formal or statement looks. The bezel setting keeps the profile flat and modern, which works across casual and formal outfits equally well.

### Should I buy marquise studs in white gold or yellow gold?

White gold is the more versatile choice — it works with any stone color and any outfit. Yellow gold is the right choice for H-I color stones (the warm metal makes the stone's warmth look intentional) or for buyers who primarily wear yellow gold jewelry. Both are identically priced at 0.25 CTW and 0.5 CTW in the bezel collection.

### What is the best way to match marquise stud earrings to a marquise pendant?

If your pendant has a 1ct stone, buy 2 CTW studs to get 1ct per ear — a true visual match. If you want the earrings to complement rather than match, buy one CTW tier below the pendant weight. A 0.5ct pendant pairs nicely with a 0.5 CTW earring set (0.25ct per ear) — the earrings are clearly smaller and serve as a supporting jewelry piece, not a competing one.

### What is the best setting for marquise diamond earrings worn every day?

Bezel, without qualification. The bezel wraps both marquise tips in metal. Nothing snags. Nothing chips. You put them in and forget about them. Prong and martini settings are beautiful for occasional wear but not appropriate for daily use with the marquise shape. The exposed tips catch fabric and hair constantly. The [bezel setting](/bezel-setting-diamond) is the professional recommendation for any marquise earring worn regularly.
